1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
One sky. Ancient China argued over its
shape.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
From the Han dynasty to the Tang: about
800 years of argument.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Three schools. The sky is an umbrella,
an egg — or no shell at all?

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Gaitian, first version: the sky is an
open canopy; the earth, a square
chessboard.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:27,000
The sun rises east, sets west. How? An
ant on a millstone.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The stone turns left, fast. The ant
crawls right, slowly — and is carried
left anyway.

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Huntian, attributed to Zhang Heng: the
sky is an egg, the earth its yolk.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,500
Water inside the shell — and the earth
floats on it.

9
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:50,500
This sphere could be built as an
instrument and used for calendars.

10
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:54,500
Cai Yong: Gaitian “often wrong”; Huntian
“close to the truth”.

11
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:58,500
The third, and boldest: Xuanye. Its
books are long lost.

12
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:04,000
One saying survives: the sky has no
substance, high and far, without end.

13
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Sun, moon and stars float in empty
space, pushed by qi. Planets speed up,
slow, turn back.

14
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
No instruments, no calendar. Cai Yong:
“no method handed down”.

15
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Its qi isn’t modern physics. But it
broke the hard shell: closer to today’s
universe than the other two.

16
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Tupi-Guarani: a sky written down in
1614.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

